              ],
              "explanation": "Query matches the concept; a source-supported STUDIES edge connects it to this experiment.",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "source_url": "https://psi.nasa.gov/geode-py/ws/studies/PSI-117/download?file=6a282739d9d7246402cb43e1&version=1&studyVersion=2&redirect=true",
                  "locator": "page 13",
                  "text": " Dynamics of near-limit cool flame burning of isolated submillimeter (Do = 0.5 mm) n-C7H16 droplet is numerically studied.  Reduced ambient O2 mole fraction (10%) is considered to facilitate near- limit condition.  Under selective O3 seeding (5%), the droplet briefly attempts to initiate stable cool flame before entering continuous oscillatory cool flame burn.  Local depletion of O2 (<13.5%) near the droplet initiates the pulsing behavior with initial small fluctuation in flame temperature.  The oscillation is found to increase in magnitude with time, and at each successive cycle.  Dynamic interaction of degenerate chain branching and chain propagation / termination reaction classes of QOOH (within NTC region) is responsible for the observed oscillation.  Continuous fuel leakage through the flame location assists the oscillation to grow in radial direction with time. Summary 13"
                }
              ]
            }
