                  "locator": "page 11",
                  "text": "11 Extinction Diameter Comparison of measured and predicted hot and cool flame extinction diameter for different initial droplet diameter for the three n-alkane fuels (atmospheric air) • High temperature combustion is strongly governed by the small species kinetics – all three fuels share nearly identical values of HF extinction diameter for the same do • The LTC differs with carbon number as each involve reactions associated with the carbon number of the fuel and the large intermediates produced. The overall rates of isomerization of radical-O2 adducts controlling rate of degenerate chain branching rate increase from C7 to C12 • The CF extinction is dictated by the increase in decomposition of •O2QnOOH for all three fuels."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "concept": "Alkane chain-length effects",
              "matched_terms": [
                "affect",
                "alkane",
                "chain",
                "cool",
                "extinction",
                "flame",
                "length"
              ],
              "explanation": "Query matches the concept; a source-supported STUDIES edge connects it to this experiment.",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "source_url": "https://psi.nasa.gov/geode-py/ws/studies/PSI-117/download?file=6a282739d9d7246402cb43e0&version=1&studyVersion=2&redirect=true",
                  "locator": "page 16",
                  "text": "16 • Two-stage hot-cool flame burning mode transitions are demonstrated to exist for the three fuels, and the maximum flame temperature at which transition to the second stage occurred was universally found to be ~ 1200K. • Hot flame characteristics of all three fuels indicate that predicted extinction phenomena are all very similar. • Cool flame extinction properties are significantly affected by the n-alkane molecular weight, and the chain length of the n-alkane has significant effect on the cool flame reactivity - larger n- alkane exhibited more robust cool flame (i.e., higher temperature) and hence smaller extinction diameters. • Universally, de,CF for each fuel varies linearly with initial droplet diameter, but unlike de,HF these relationships do not collapse to a single function. • n-Dodecane droplet combustion reveal a strong dependence of observations on ambient pressure. Single, two-stage hot-cool flame combustion transitions to a multi-cycle combustion behavior at ambient pressures above one atmosphere, and eventually into a hot - “Warm” flame transition. Summary"
                }
              ]
            }
