        {
          "node": {
            "id": "exp:PSI-117:5093a070c957f0581ff9",
            "type": "Experiment",
            "properties": {
              "title": "Preferential vaporization in multicomponent Jet-A surrogate droplets",
              "summary": "The accessed paper abstract describes numerical studies of three multicomponent Jet-A surrogates with different distillation properties. A one-dimensional transient model uses reduced kinetics, multicomponent diffusion and UNIFAC liquid activity coefficients. Comparisons with published 550 µm drop-tower data support mixture-1 predictions; the authors report nonlinear dependence of preferential vaporization on droplet size and pressure. This extraction is grounded in the accessible abstract, not an assertion of full-text access.",
              "keywords": [
                "preferential vaporization",
                "Jet-A",
                "surrogate fuel",
                "UNIFAC",
                "batch distillation"
              ],
              "conditions": {
                "mixture_1_molar_ratio": "n-decane/iso-octane/toluene 42.7/33.0/24.3",
                "mixture_2_molar_ratio": "n-dodecane/iso-octane/1,3,5-trimethylbenzene 49.0/21.0/30.0",
                "mixture_3_molar_ratio": "n-hexadecane/iso-octane/1,3,5-trimethylbenzene 36.5/31.0/32.5",
                "validation_diameter": "550 µm",
                "validation_ambient": "air",
                "study_kind": "numerical, compared with published drop-tower data"
              },
              "methods": [
                "Transient one-dimensional sphero-symmetric model",
                "Reduced detailed kinetics",
                "Multicomponent gas diffusion",
                "UNIFAC liquid activity coefficients",
                "Stagnant versus internally mixed liquid models"
              ],
              "source_url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.proci.2020.06.200"
            }
          },
          "score": 0.3736,
          "reasons": [
            {
              "concept": "Preferential vaporization",
              "matched_terms": [
                "burning"
              ],
              "explanation": "Query matches the concept; a source-supported STUDIES edge connects it to this experiment.",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "source_url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.proci.2020.06.200",
                  "locator": "HTML body",
                  "text": "Proceedings of the Combustion Institute Date: 2021 Page s : 3313-3323 Volume: Volume 38, Issue 2 View accessibility information Published by: Elsevier Published by Show more Research article Full text access Get rights and content Sub-millimeter sized multi-component jet fuel surrogate droplet combustion: Physicochemical preferential vaporization effects Author links open overlay panel Tanvir I. Farouk , Sang Hee Won , Frederick L. Dryer Show more View PDF Download full issue View Open Manuscript Cite Add to Mendeley Share 10.1016/j.proci.2020.06.200 More actions Abstract Isolated droplet burning behaviors of fuel mixtures that all share the fully pre-vaporized combustion behaviors of the same “global” Jet-A real fuel are investigated numerically to elucidate the effects of preferential vaporization. Predictions are generated using three such multi-component hydrocarbon mixtures (Mixture-1: n-decane/iso-octane/toluene 42.7/33.0/24.3, Mixture-2: n-dodecane/iso-octane/1,3,5 trimethyl benzene 49.0/21.0/30.0 and Mixture-3: n-hexadecane/iso-octane/1,3,5 trimethyl benzene 36.5/31.0/32.5 molar ratios), each having very different distillation properties. Simulations are performed using a transient one-dimensional sphero-symmetric model, involving numerically reduced detailed chemical kinetics, and multi-component gas-phase diffusive transport. The interactions among the different liquid-phase components are modeled using UNIFAC activity coefficient methodology. The predictions using Mixture-1 are found to be in good agreement with previously published drop-tower experiments for 550 μm droplet combustion in air. Stagnant and internally mixed liquid-phase behaviors are both considered. Near fully mixed internal conditions and including sooting effects (observed in the experiments) result in predictions that are in good agreement with experimental drop diameter and flame-standoff ratio histories. By comparing predictions for the three mixtures, preferential vaporization effects exhibit strong non-linear dependences on initial droplet diameter and ambient pressure. At low pressures and for droplet sizes typical of those found in multi-phase gas turbine combustors, comparable diffusion and vaporization characteristic times favor frozen limit vaporization. However, at pressures typical of those found in applications, batch distillation dominates and preferential vaporization chemical property differences are found to become as significant as physical property effects in influencing combustion behaviors. Keywords Preferential vaporization ; Droplet combustion ; Multi-component ; Surrogate fuel View Abstract Recommended articles No articles found. © 2020 The Combustion Institute. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved."
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
