                  "locator": "page 28",
                  "text": "Effect of buoyancy a) Temperature distribution and streamlines, and b) heat release rate, for DME diffusion cool flame with and without buoyancy for identical conditions (XDME= 0.587, UF = 62.3 cm/s, UO = 50.9 cm/s, a ~ 90 s-1, P = 1 atm)  Buoyancy lifts the flame upward.  Without buoyancy, 1D and 2D models predict the flame at or near the stagnation plane due to momentum- balanced BC.  The peak flame temperature in 1D is slightly lower compared to the 2D prediction, but the peak HRR shows the opposite trend.  Temperature gradient is higher in 1D calculation. More diffusive heat loss."
                },
                {
                  "source_url": "https://psi.nasa.gov/geode-py/ws/studies/PSI-117/download?file=6a282739d9d7246402cb43df&version=1&studyVersion=2&redirect=true",
                  "locator": "page 29",
                  "text": "Flow field analyses c d Computed centerline distribution of a) axial velocity, b) axial velocity gradient Computed radial distribution of c) axial, and d) radial velocity at the fuel-side nozzle exit  The extended entrance channels allow the velocity at the nozzle exits to be perturbed by the flame.  2D model captures the perturbation in a self-consistent manner.  2D model without buoyancy retains the momentum-balanced condition and forms the stagnation plane at the axis midpoint, exhibiting identical perturbation on either side  Axisymmetric dip in 2D simulation, observed in experiments of Sarnecki et al."
                }
