                  "locator": "page 7",
                  "text": "7 High Pressure Helium Exchange – 3 atm Evolution of droplet diameter (n-heptane, do = 4.00 mm, XO2 = 21%, He exchange with balance N2, 3 atm). Evolution of peak temperature (n-heptane, do = 4.00 mm, XO2 = 21%, He exchange with balance N2, 3 atm). Introduction of helium increases the burning rate, at higher helium concentration a third stage quasi-steady burn is observed 1st Stage High Temperature burn 2nd Stage Moderate Temperature burn Tflame ~ 950 K 3rd Stage Low Temperature burn Tflame ~ 770 K"
                },
                {
                  "source_url": "https://psi.nasa.gov/geode-py/ws/studies/PSI-117/download?file=6a282739d9d7246402cb43e2&version=1&studyVersion=2&redirect=true",
                  "locator": "page 17",
                  "text": "17 Summary • The experimental results and detailed predictions support that there exists two possible modes of quasi-steady cool flame droplet burning behaviour: “Warm” in the NTC regime and “Cold” in the low temperature regime. • The helium concentration is critical for the observed characteristics – promoting the second stage burn or completely supressing it. • A dynamic coupling between heat generation and heat loss in the system. • The low temperature kinetics is distinctive and different in the second and third stage. • Experimental observation with n-dodecane at higher pressure and helium exchange validates the “a priori” numerical predictions of a three stage behaviour inferred from n-heptane simulations."
                }
              ]
